\id 1PE Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Peter
\toc1 The First Epistle of Peter the Apostle.
\toc2 1 Peter
\toc3 1PE
\mt1 The First Epistle of Peter the Apostle.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF BOTH THE EPISTLES OF S. PETER, THE FIRST, AND THE SECOND.
\ip OF S. Peter we reade at large, both in the Goſpels, and in the Actes of the Apoſtles: and namely, that Chriſt deſigned him, and alſo made him his vicar (as S. Mathew \f - \ft \+it c. 10. v. 2.\+it*\f* for that cauſe in the catalogue of the Apoſtles, called him \it Primus, the firſt\it* , and all antiquitie, \it Princeps Apoſtolorum, the Prince of the Apoſtles\it* ) and that he accordingly executed that office after Chriſtes departure, plãting the Church firſt among the Jews in Hieruſalem and in al that countrey and coaſtes about, as Chriſt alſo himſelf before had preached to the Jews alone.
\ip But preaching at length to the Gentiles alſo, according to Chriſtes commiſſion (Mat. 28. v. 19.) and being now come to Rome, the head citie of the Gentiles, from thence he writeth this Epiſtle to his Chriſtian Jews, hauing care of them in his abſence, no leſſe then when he was preſent: and not to the Jews that were at home, (belike becauſe they had S. Iames, or his ſucceſſor S. Simon Cleophæ, reſident with them) but \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 1.\+it*\f* to them that were diſperſed in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, and Bithynia.
\ip And that he writeth it from Rome, himſelf ſignifieth ſaying: \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 5.\+it*\f* \it The Church that is in Babylon ſaluteth you\it* . \f - \ft * See the Annotation \+it 1. Pet. 5. v. 13.\+it*\f* Where by Babylon he meaneth Rome, as al antiquitie doth interpret him: not, that he ſo calleth the Church of Rome, but the heathen ſtate of the Romane empire, which then, and 300 yeres after, vnto the conuerſion of Conſtantinus the Emperour, did perſecute the elect Church of Rome, in ſo much that the firſt 33 Biſhops thereof vnto S. Silueſter, were al Martyrs.
\ip For the matter whereof he writeth, himſelf doth ſignifie it in theſe wordes: \it This loe the ſecond Epiſtle I write to you, my deereſt, in Which (Epiſtles\it* \f - \ft \+it 2. Pet. 3.\+it*\f* \it ) I ſtirre vp by admonition, your ſincere minde, that you may be mindeful of thoſe wordes &c.\it* So he ſaith there of both together. And againe of the firſt to the ſame purpoſe, in another place: \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 5.\+it*\f* \it I haue breefely written, beſeeching and teſtifying that this is the true grace of God, wherein you ſtand\it* . For, there were at that time certaine Seducers (as \f - \ft * See the Annotation vpon S. Iames epiſtle \+it c. 2. v. 21.\+it*\f* S. Auguſt. alſo hath told vs) who went about to teach \it Onely faith\it* , as though good workes were not neceſſarie, nor meritorious. There were alſo great perſecutions, to compel them with terrour to denie Chriſt & al his religi. He therfore exhorteth them accordingly, neither for perſecution, neither by ſeduction to forſake it: though in the firſt, his exhortation is more principally againſt perſecution: and in the ſecond, more principally againſt ſeduction. The firſt epiſtle is noted to be very like to S. Paules epiſtle to the Epheſians, in wordes alſo, and ſo thicke of Scriptures, as though he ſpake nothing els.
\ip The time when the firſt was written, is vncertaine: the ſecond was written a litle before his death, as is gathered by his wordes in the ſame. c. 1. v. 14.
\c 1
\cd He comforteth them in their perſecutions ( being now by Baptiſme made the children of God ) vvith the hope of their heauenly inheritance: 6 ſhewing how meritorious it is for them to be ſo conſtant in faith, 10 and confirming them therein vvith the authoritie of the Prophets and of the Holy Ghoſt. 15 Exhorting them to liue alſo accordingly in al holines, 15 conſidering the holines of God, the vprightnes of his iudgement, the price of their redemption by Chriſt, 22 and the vertue of the ſeede in them ( Which is grace regeneratiue in Baptiſme ) foretold by the Prophet alſo.
\p
\v 1 \sc PETER\sc* \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The Epiſtle \+it In Cathedra S. Petri Rome.\+it* Ian. 18. \+it & Antiochiæ\+it* Feb. 22.\f* an Apoſtle of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, to the electe ſtrangers of the diſperſion of Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Aſia, and Bithynia,
\v 2 according to the preſcience of God the Father, into ſanctification of the Spirit, vnto the obedience and ſprinkling of the bloud of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt: Grace to you and peace be multiplied.
\v 3 \x - \xt 2. Cor. 1, 3. Eph. 1, 3.\x*\f 2 \fr 1.3 \ft The Epiſtle for many martyrs.\f* Bleſſed be God and the father of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, vvho according to his great mercie hath regenerated vs vnto a liuely hope, by the reſurrection of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt from the dead,
\v 4 vnto an inheritance incorruptible, and incontaminate, and that can not fade, conſerued in the heauens in you,
\v 5 (vvho in the vertue of God are kept by faith vnto ſaluation) ready to be reuealed in the laſt time.
\v 6 wherin you ſhal reioyce, a litle novv if you muſt be made heauy in diuerſe tentations:
\v 7 that the probation of your faith much more pretious then gold (vvhich is proued by the fire) may be found vnto praiſe and glorie and honour in the reuelation of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt: ⊢
\v 8 vvhom hauing not ſeen, you loue: in vvhom novv alſo not ſeing you beleeue: and beleeuing you reioyce vvith ioy vnſpeakable and glorified,
\v 9 receiuing the end of your faith, the ſaluation of your ſoules.
\v 10 Of the vvhich ſaluation the Prophetes inquired & ſearched, vvhich prophecied of the grace to come in you,
\v 11 ſearching vnto vvhich or vvhat maner of time the Spirit of Chriſt in them did ſignifie: foretelling thoſe paſſions that are in Chriſt and the glories folovving:
\v 12 to vvhom it vvas reuealed, that not to thē ſelues, but to you they miniſtred thoſe things vvhich novv are told you by them that haue euangelized to you, the holy Ghoſt being ſent from heauen, on vvhom the Angels deſire to looke.
\v 13 For the vvhich cauſe hauing the loines of your \f 1 \fr 1.13 \ft Chaſtitie not onely of body but alſo of minde, is required. \+it S. Bede vpon this place\+it*.\f* minde girded, ſober, truſt perfectly in that grace vvhich is offered you, in the reuelation of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt,
\v 14 as children of obedience, not configurated to the former deſires of your ignorance:
\v 15 but according to him that hath called you, the Holy one, be you alſo in al conuerſation holy:
\v 16 becauſe it is vvritten: \it You ſhal be holy, becauſe I am holy\it*. \x - \xt Lev. 11, 19. 20, 7. Deu. 10. Ro. 2. Gal. 2.\x*
\v 17 And if you inuocate the Father, him vvhich vvithout acception of perſons iudgeth according to euery ones \f 1 \fr 1.17 \ft Deu. 10. Ro. 2. Gal. 2. · God vvil iudge men according to euery ones vvorkes, and not by faith onely.\f* vvorke: in feare conuerſe ye the time of your peregrination.
\v 18 Knovving that \x - \xt 1. Cor. 6, 20. 7, 23.\x* not vvith corruptible things, gold or ſiluer, you are redeemed from your vaine conuerſation of your fathers \f 2 \fr 1.18 \ft 1 Cor. 6, 20. 7, 23. · He meaneth the errours of Gentility. or if he vvrite to the Iewes diſperſed, he meaneth the yoke of the Law vvith the fond and heauy additions of their later Maiſters, called \+it Deuterôſes\+it*. The Heretikes, to make it ſound to the ſimple againſt the traditions of the Churche, corrupt the text thus, \+it Which you haue receiued by tradition of the fathers\+it*.\f* tradition:
\v 19 but vvith the precious bloud as it vvere of an immaculate and vnſpotted lambe, Chriſt,
\v 20 \x - \xt Ro. 16, 25. Col. 1, 26. Tit. 1, 2.\x* foreknovven in deede before the conſtitution of the vvorld, but manifeſted in the laſt times for you,
\v 21 vvhich by him are faithful in God vvho raiſed him from the dead, and hath giuen him glorie, that your faith and hope might be in God.
\v 22 Making your ſoules chaſte in obedience of charitie, in the ſincere loue of the fraternitie from the hart loue ye one an other earneſtly:
\v 23 borne againe not of corruptible ſeede, but incorruptible by the vvord of God vvho liueth and remaineth for euer.
\v 24 For \x - \xt Eſa. 40, 6.\x* \it al fleſh is as graſſe: and al the glorie thereof as the floure of graſſe. the graſſe is vvithered, and the floure thereof is fallen avvay\it*.
\v 25 But the vvord of our Lord remaineth for euer, and this is the vvord that is euangelized among you.
\c 2
\cd Novv after their Baptiſme, vvhat muſt be their meate: 4 and being come to Chriſt, hovv happie they be aboue their incredulous brethren, according to the Scriptures alſo. 11 Wherevpon he beſeecheth them to ſhine in good life among the Heathen, ſo to procure their conuerſion: 13 to be obedient ſubiects to higher Povvers ( hovvſoeuer ſome miſconſter Chriſtian libertie ) 14 and ſeruants to obey their Maiſters. 19 And ſo, doing vvel, though they ſuffer for it, it is very meritorious, 21 vvhereas Chriſt alſo not onely gaue them example, 24 but alſo by his death hath made them able to liue iuſtly.
\p
\v 1 \sc LAYING\sc* \f 1 \fr 2.1 \ft The Epiſtle upon Saturday in Eaſter week.\f* avvay therfore al malice, and al guile, and ſimulations, and enuies, and al detractions,
\v 2 as infants euen novv borne, reaſonable, milke vvithout guile deſire ye, that in it you may grovv vnto ſaluation.
\v 3 if yet you haue taſted that our Lord is ſvveete.
\v 4 Vnto vvhom approching, a liuing ſtone, of men in deede reprobated, but of God elect and made honorable:
\v 5 be ye alſo your ſelues ſuperedified as it vvere liuing ſtones, \f 1 \fr 2.5 \fq `ſpiritual houſes \ft \+it `a ſpiritual houſe\+it*,\f* `ſpiritual houſes', a holy prieſthod, to offer ſpiritual hoſtes, acceptable to God by \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt. \ef - \fr 2.5 \fq Spiritual hoſtes. \ft ¹ Here vve ſee, that as he ſpeaketh of ſpiritual hoſtes, vvhich euery Chriſtian man offereth, ſo he ſpeaketh not properly of prieſthod, vvhen he maketh al Prieſts, but of a ſpiritual prieſthod. Which ſpiritual prieſthod vvas alſo in al the Iewes: but the prieſthod ( properly ſo called ) vvas onely in the ſonnes of Aaron, and they offered the ſacrifices ( properly ſo called ) vvhich none beſides might offer. \fq ¹ \ft Spiritual hostes and Priests.\ef*
\v 6 For the vvhich cauſe the Scripture conteineth, \x - \xt Eſa. 28, 16.\x* \it Behold I put in Sion a principal corner ſtone elect, pretious. and he that ſhal beleeue in him, ſhal not be confounded\it*.
\v 7 To you therfore that beleeue, honour: but to them that beleeue not, \x - \xt Ps. 117. Mt. 21. Act. 4. Eſa. 8. Ro. 9, 33. Exo. 19. Apoc. 1.\x* \it the ſtone vvhich the builders reiected, the ſame is made into the head of the corner\it*:
\v 8 and a ſtone of offenſe, and a rocke of ſcandal, to them that ſtumble at the vvord, neither doe beleeue \f 1 \fr 2.8 \fq `vvherin alſo they are put \ft \+it `vvhereto alſo they are ordeined\+it*.\f* `vvherin alſo they are put'.
\v 9 But you are an elect generation, a \f 1 \fr 2.9 \ft The Proteſtants can no more gather of this, that al Chriſtians be Prieſts: then, that al be kings: as is moſt plaine \+it Apocalyp. 1, 6. and 5, 10. Thou haſt made vs a kingdom ( or kings ) & prieſts\+it*.\f* ckingly prieſthod, a holy nation, a people of purchaſe: that you may declare his vertues vvhich from darkeneſſe hath called you into his maruelous light.
\v 10 \x - \xt Oſe. 2. Ro. 9.\x* \it Vvhich ſometime not a people: but novv the people of God. Vvhich not hauing obtained mercie: but novv hauing obtained mercie\it*.
\v 11 \x - \xt Gal. 5, 16.\x* My deereſt I beſeche you as ſtrangers & pilgrimes, \f 2 \fr 2.11 \ft The Epiſtle upon the 3 Sunday after Eaſter.\f* to refraine your ſelues from carnal deſires which vvarre againſt the ſoule,
\v 12 hauing your conuerſation good among the Gentiles: that in that vvherein they miſreport of you as of malefactors, by the good vvorkes conſidering you, \x - \xt Mt. 5, 16.\x* they may glorifie God in the day of viſitation.
\v 13 \x - \xt Ro. 13, 1.\x* Be ſubiect therfore to euery \f 2 \fr 2.13 \ft So is the Greeke. but the Proteſt. in fauour of temporal lavves made againſt the Cath. religion, tranſlate it very falſely thus, \+it to al maner ordinance of man\+it*: thē ſelues boldly reiecting Eccleſiaſtical decrees as mens ordinances. · κτίσει · Ro. 13, 1.\f* humane \f 3 \fr 2.13 \ft \+it c κτίσει.\+it*\f* creature for God: vvhether it be to king, as excelling: \ef - \fr 2.13 \fq Be ſubiect. \ft ¹ Not onely our Maiſter Chriſt, but the Apoſtles and al Chriſtians vvere euer charged by ſuch as thought to bring them in hatred vvith Princes, vvith diſobedience to kings and temporal Magiſtrates. therfore both ² S. Paul and this Apoſtle do ſpecially vvarne the faithful, that they giue no occaſion by their il demeanure to ſecular Princes, that the Heathen ſhould count them diſobedient or ſeditious vvorkers againſt the States of the vvorld. \fq ¹ \ft Obedience to temporal princes. \fq ² \ft \+it Ro. 13.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.13 \fq To euery humane creature. \ft So he calleth the temporal Magiſtrate elected by the people, or holding their Souerainty by birth & carnal propagation, ordained for the vvorldly vvealth, peace, and proſperitie of the ſubiects: to put a difference betvvixt that humane Superiority, and the ſpiritual Rulers and regiment, guiding and gouerning the people to an higher end, and inſtituted ¹ by God him ſelf immediatly. for Chriſt did expreſly conſtitute the forme of regiment vſed euer ſince in the Church. He made one the cheefe, placing Peter in the Supremacie: he called the Apoſtles and Diſciples, giuing them their ſeueral authorities. Aftervvard ² God guided the lot for choiſe of S. Matthias in Iudas place: and the Holy Ghoſt expreſly and namely ſeuered and choſe Paul and Barnabas vnto their Apoſtolical function: and generally the Apoſtle ſaith of al ſpiritual Rulers, \+it The holy Ghoſt hath placed you to rule the Church of God\+it*. ³And although al povver be of God, and kings rule by him, yet that is no othervviſe, but by his ordinarie concurrence, and prouidence, vvhereby he procureth the earthly commodity or vvealth of men, by maintaining of due ſuperiority and ſubiection one tovvards an other, and by giuing povver to the people and Commonvvealth to chooſe to them ſelues ſome kinde or forme of Regiment, vnder vvhich they be content to liue for their preſeruation in peace and tranquillity. But Spiritual ſuperiority is far more excellent, as in more excellent ſort depending, not of mans ordinance, election, or ( as this Apoſtle ſpeaketh ) creation, but of the Holy Ghoſt, vvho is alvvaies reſident in the Church ( vvhich is Chriſts body myſtical, and therfore an other manner of Commonwealth then the earthly ) concurring in ſingular ſort to the creation of al neceſſarie Officers in the ſaid Church, euen to the vvorlds end, as S. Paul vvriteth to the Epheſians. ⁴Leſt therfore the people, being then in ſo preciſe ſort alvvaies vvarned of the excellencie of their Spiritual gouernours ⁵ and of their obedience tovvard them, might neglect their dueties to Temporal Magiſtrates, ſpecially being infidels, and many times tyrants and perſecutors of the faith, as Nero and other vvere then: therfore S. Peter here vvarneth them to be ſubiect, for their bodies and goods and other temporal things, euen to the vvorldly Princes both infidels and Chriſtians, vvhom he calleth humane creatures. \fq ¹ \ft God instituted the Spiritual gouernement in more excellent maner then the temporal. \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 20.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Eph. 4\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Hebr. 13.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.13 \fq To the king as excelling. \ft Some ſimple heretikes & others alſo not vnlearned, at the begining, for lacke of better places, vvould haue proued by this, that the king vvas head of the Church, and aboue al Spiritual rulers: and to make it ſound better that vvay, they falſely tranſlated ¹ it, \+it To the king as to the cheefe head\+it*. in the Bible of the yere 1562. But it is euident that he calleth the king, the precellent or more excellent, ² in reſpect of his Vicegerents vvhich he calleth Dukes or Gouernours that be at his appointment: and not in reſpect of Popes, Biſhops, or Prieſts, as they haue the rule of mens ſoules: vvho could not in that charge be vnder ſuch Kings or Emperours as the Apoſtle ſpeaketh of: no more then the kings or Emperours then, could be heads of the Church, being Heathen men and no members thereof, much leſſe the cheefe members. See a notable place in S. Ignatius ep. ad Smyrnenſes, vvhere he exhorteth them firſt to honour God, next the Biſhop, & then the king. ³ This is an inuincible demonſtration, that this text maketh not for any ſpiritual claime of earthly kings, becauſe it giueth no more to any Prince then may and ought to be done and graunted to a Heathen Magiſtrate. Neither is there any thing in al the nevv Teſtament that proueth the Prince to be head or cheefe gouernour of the Church in ſpiritual or Eccleſiaſtical cauſes, more then it proueth any heathen Emperour of Rome to haue been. for they vvere bound in temporal things to obey the heathen being lavvful kings, to be ſubiect to them, euen for conſcience, to keepe their temporal lavves, to pay them tribute, to pray for them, and to doe al other natural duties: and more no ſcriptures binde vs to doe to Chriſtian kinges. \fq ¹ \ft Heret. translation. \fq ² \ft The Kings excellencie of power is in respect of the nobilitie and lay magistrates vnder him. \fq ³ \ft Christian Princes haue no more right to be supreme heads in spiritual causes, then the Heathen.\ef*
\v 14 or to rulers as ſent by him to the reuenge of malefactors, but to the praiſe of the good:
\v 15 for ſo is the vvil of God, that doing vvel you may make the ignorance of vnvviſe men to be dumme:
\v 16 as free, & not as hauing the freedom for a cloke of malice, but as the ſeruants of God. \ef - \fr 2.16 \fq Not as hauing. \ft There vvere ſome Libertines in thoſe daies, as there be novv, that vnder pretence of libertie of the Goſpel, ¹ ſought to be free from ſubiection and lawes of men as now vnder the like vvicked pretence, Heretikes refuſe to obey their ſpiritual rulers and to obſerue their lawes. \fq ¹ \ft Libertines.\ef*
\v 17 Honour al men. \f 1 \fr 2.17 \ft In this ſpeache is often commended the vnitie of al Chriſtians among them ſelues.\f* Loue the fraternitie. Feare God. Honour the king.
\v 18 Seruants be ſubiect in al feare to your maiſters, not only to the good & modeſt, but alſo to the vvaivvard. \ef - \fr 2.18 \fq But alſo the vvaivvard. ). \ft ¹ The Vviclefiſtes and their folovvers in theſe daies, ſometimes to moue the people vnto ſedition, hold and teach that maiſters and magiſtrates loſe their authoritie ouer their ſeruants and ſubiects, if they be once in deadly ſinne, and that the people in that caſe neede not in conſcience obey them. Vvhich is a pernicious and falſe doctrine, as is plaine by this place, vvhere vve be expreſly commaunded to obey euen the il-conditioned. vvhich muſt be alvvaies vnderſtood, if they commaund nothing againſt God. for then this rule is euer to be folovved. \+it Vve muſt obey God rather then men\+it*. Act. 5, 29. \fq ¹ \ft Deadly sinnes of Princes or Superiors exempt not the subiectes from obedience, as Wicleffe held.\ef*
\v 19 For this is thanke, if for conſcience of God a man ſuſtaine ſorovves, ſuffering vniuſtly.
\v 20 For vvhat glorie is it: if ſinning, and buffeted you ſuffer? but if doing vvel you ſuſtaine patiently: this is thanke before God.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 2.21 \ft The Epiſtle upon the 2 Sunday after Eaſter.\f* For vnto this are you called: becauſe Chriſt alſo ſuffred for \f 2 \fr 2.21 \fq `vs \ft you\f* `vs', leauing \f 3 \fr 2.21 \fq `you \ft \+it vs\+it*\f* `you' an example that you may folovv his ſteppes.
\v 22 \x - \xt Eſa. 53, 9.\x* \it vvho did no ſinne, neither vvas guile found in his mouth\it*.
\v 23 vvho vvhen he vvas reuiled, \f 1 \fr 2.23 \ft c ὀυκ ἀντελοιδόρει.\f* did not reuile: vvhen he ſuffred, he threatened not: but deliuered him ſelf to him that iudged him vniuſtly.
\v 24 vvho him ſelf \x - \xt Eſa. 53, 4. Mt. 8, 17.\x* bare our ſinnes in his body vpon the tree: that dead to ſinnes, we may liue to iuſtice. by vvhoſe ſtripes you are healed.
\v 25 For you vvere as ſheepe ſtraying: but you be conuerted novv to the Paſtor and Biſhop of your ſoules. ⊢
\c 3
\cd The dutie of vviues and huſbands to ech other. 9 None to doe or ſpeake euil by their perſecutors, 15 but to anſvver them alvvaies vvith modeſtie, and ſpecially vvith innocencie, after the example of Chriſt moſt innocent: vvhoſe body though they killed, yet his ſoule liued and preached aftervvard to the ſoules in Hel ( namely to thoſe in the time of Noës floud being a figure of our Baptiſme ) roſe againe, and aſcended.
\p
\v 1 IN \x - \xt Eph. 5, 28. Col. 3, 18.\x* like maner alſo \f 2 \fr 3.1 \ft How huſbands ſhould behave themſelves toward their wives.\f* let the vvomen be ſubiect to their huſbandes: that if any beleeue not the vvord, by the conuerſation of the vvomen vvithout the vvord they may be vvonne,
\v 2 conſidering your chaſt conuerſation in feare.
\v 3 \x - \xt 1. Tim. 2, 9.\x*\f 2 \fr 3.3 \ft Againſt the proud, curious and coſtly attire of vvomē, vvherein this il time of ours exceedeth.\f* VVhoſe trimming let it not be outvvardly the plaiting of heare, or laying on gold round about, or of putting on veſtures:
\v 4 but the man of the hart that is hidden, in the incorruptibilitie of a quiet and a modeſt ſpirit, vvhich is riche in the ſight of God.
\v 5 For ſo ſometime the holy vvomen alſo that truſted in God, adorned them ſelues, ſubiect to their ovvne huſbandes.
\v 6 As \x - \xt Gen. 18.\x* Sara obeied Abraham, calling him lord: \f 2 \fr 3.6 \ft \+it c ἧς\+it*\f* vvhoſe daughters you are, doing vvel, and not fearing any perturbation.
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 3.7 \ft How huſbands ſhould behave themſelves toward their wives.\f* Huſbandes likevviſe, dvvelling vvith them according to knovvledge, as vnto the vveaker feminine veſſel imparting honour, as it vvere to the coheires alſo of the grace of life: that your praiers be not hindered.
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 3.8 \ft The Epiſtle upon the 5. Sunday after Pentecoſt.\f* And \f 2 \fr 3.8 \fq `in fine \ft \+it `in faith\+it*\f* `in fine' al of one minde, hauing compaſſion, louers of the fraternitie, merciful, modeſt, humble.
\v 9 \x - \xt Pro. 17, 13. Mat. 5, 44.\x* not rendering euil for euil, nor curſe for curſe: but contrariewiſe, bleſſing: for vnto this are you called, that you may by inheritance poſſeſſe a benediction.
\v 10 \x - \xt Pſ. 33, 13.\x* \it For he that vvil loue life, and ſee good daies: let him refraine his tong from euil, and his lippes that they ſpeake not guile\it*.
\v 11 \it Let him decline from euil, and doe good: let him enquire peace, and folovv it\it*:
\v 12 \it becauſe the eies of our Lord are vpon the iuſt, and his eares vnto their praiers: but the countenance of our Lord vpon them that doe euil things\it*.
\v 13 And vvho is he that can hurt you, if you be emulators of good?
\v 14 But \x - \xt Mat. 5, 10.\x* & if you ſuffer ought for iuſtice, bleſſed are ye. And the feare of them feare ye not, & be not troubled.
\v 15 But ſanctifie our Lord Chriſt in your hartes, ready alwaies to ſatiſfie euery one that aſketh you a reaſon of that hope vvhich is in you:
\v 16 but vvith modeſtie and feare, hauing a good conſcience: that in that vvhich they ſpeake il of you, they may be confounded vvhich calumniate your good conuerſation in Chriſt.
\v 17 For it is better to ſuffer as doing vvel (if the vvil of God vvil haue it ſo) then doing il.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 3.18 \ft The Epiſtle vpon friday in Eaſter vveeke.\f* Becauſe Chriſt alſo died once for our ſinnes, the iuſt for the vniuſt: that he might offer vs to God, mortified certes in fleſh, but quickened in ſpirit.
\v 19 In the vvhich ſpirit comming he preached to \f 1 \fr 3.19 \fq `them \ft \+it `thoſe ſpirites\+it*\f* `them' alſo that vvere in priſon: \ef - \fr 3.19 \fq To them that vvere in priſon. \ft S. Auguſtine in his 99 Epiſtle \+it in principio\+it*, confeſſeth this place to be exceding hard to vnderſtand, & to haue many difficulties vvhich he could neuer explicate to his ovvne ſatisfaction. Yet vnto Heretikes this and al other textes be eaſie, not doubting but that is the ſenſe vvhich them ſelues imagin, vvhatſoeuer other men deeme thereof. ¹ S. Auguſtine onely findeth him ſelf ſure of this, that Chriſts deſcending into Hel in ſoule after his death, is plainely proued hereby. Vvhich thing he declareth there, to be conformable to diuers other expreſſe vvordes of holy Vvrite, and namely to this ſame Apoſtles ſermon Act. 2. And at length he concludeth thus, \+it Quis ergo niſi infidelis negauerit fuiſſe apud inferos Chriſtum\+it*? that is, \+it Therfore vvho but an infidel, vvil deny that Chriſt vvas in Hel\+it*? Caluin ² then (you ſee) vvith al his folovvers are infidels, vvho in ſteede of this deſcending of Chriſt in ſoule after his death, haue inuented an other deſperate kinde of Chriſts being in Hel, vvhen he vvas yet aliue on the Croſſe. S. Athanaſius alſo in his epiſtle cited by S. Epiphanius \+it hær. 77 in principio\+it*, and in his booke \+it de Incarnatione Verbi propius initio\+it*, S. Cyril \+it de recta fide ad Theodoſium\+it*, Oecumenius, and diuers others vpon this place, proue Chriſts deſcending to Hel. As they likevviſe declare vpon the vvordes folovving, that he preached to the ſpirites or ſoules of men deteined in Hel or in Priſon. But vvhether this vvord \+it Priſon\+it* or \+it Hel\+it* be meant of the inferiour place of the damned, or of \+it Limbus patrum\+it* called Abrahams boſome, or ſome other place of temporal chaſtiſement: and, to vvhom he preached there, and vvho by his preaching or preſence there vvere deliuered, and vvho they vvere that are called, \+it Incredulous in the daies of Noë\+it*: ³ al theſe things S. Auguſtine calleth great profundities, confeſſing him ſelf to be vnable to reache vnto it: onely holding faſt and aſſured this article of our faith, that he deliuered none deputed to damnation in the lovvest hel, and yet not doubting but that he releaſed diuers out of places of paines there. vvhich can not be out of any other place then Purgatorie. ⁴See the ſaid Epiſtle, vvhere alſo he inſinuateth other expoſitions for explication of the manifold difficulties of this hard text, vvhich vvere to long to reherſe, our ſpecial purpoſe being onely to note briefely the things that touche the controuerſies of this time. \fq ¹ \ft Christ in soule descended vnto hel, vvhiles his body lay in the graue. \fq ² \ft The Caluinists denying the same, are ( by S. Augustines iudgement ) infidels. \fq ³ \ft Certaine difficulties vvhereof S. Augustine doubteth. \fq ⁴ \ft Purgatorie.\ef*
\v 20 vvhich had been incredulous ſometime, \x - \xt Gen. 6. Mat. 24.\x* vvhen they expected the patience of God in the daies of Noë, vvhen the arke vvas a building: in the vvhich, fevv, that is, \x - \xt Gen. 7, 7.\x* eight ſoules vvere ſaued by vvater. \ef - \fr 3.20 \fq Incredulous ſometime. \ft ¹ They that take the former vvordes, of Chriſts deſcending to Hel, and deliuering certaine there detained, do expound this, not of ſuch as died in their infidelitie or vvithout al faith in God, for ſuch vvere not deliuered: but either of ſome that once vvere incredulous, and aftervvard repented before their death: or rather and ſpecially of ſuch as othervviſe vvere faithful, but yet truſted not Noës preaching by his vvorke and vvord, that God vvould deſtroy the vvorld by vvater. Vvho yet being othervviſe good men, vvhen the matter came to paſſe, vvere ſorie for their errour, and died by the floud corporally, but yet in ſtate of ſaluation. and being chaſtiſed for their fault in the next life, vvere deliuered by Chriſts deſcending thither. and not they onely, but al others in the like conditió. For the Apoſtle giueth theſe of Noes time but for an example. \fq ¹ \ft Vvhat vvere the incredulous persons of vvhom the Apostle here speaketh.\ef*
\v 21 Vvherevnto Baptiſme being of the like forme novv ſaueth \f 1 \fr 3.21 \fq `you \ft \+it vs\+it*\f* `you' alſo: not the laying avvay of the filth of the fleſh, but the examination of a good conſcience tovvard God by the reſurrection of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt. \ef - \fr 3.21 \fq Of the like forme. \ft ¹ The vvater bearing vp the Arke from ſinking, and the perſons in it from drowning, vvas a figure of Baptiſme, that likevviſe ſaueth the vvorthie receiuers from euerlaſting periſhing: \+it As Noë\+it* (ſaith S. Auguſtine) \+it vvith his, vvas deliuered by the vvater and the vvood, ſo the familie of Chriſt by Baptiſme ſigned vvith Chriſts Paſſion on the Croſſe. Li.12. Cont. Fauſtum c. 14\+it*. Againe he ſaith, ²that as the vvater ſaued none out of the Arke, but vvas rather their deſtruction: ſo the Sacrament of Baptiſme receiued out of the Catholike Church at Heretikes or Schiſmatikes hands, though it be the ſame vvater and Sacrament that the Catholike Church hath, yet profiteth none to ſaluation, but rather vvorketh their perdition. ³ Vvhich yet is not meant in caſe of extreme neceſsitie, vvhen the partie ſhould die vvithout the ſaid Sacrament, except he tooke it at an Heretikes or Schiſmatikes hand. Neither is it meant in the caſe of infants, to vvhom the Sacrament is cauſe of ſaluation, they being in no fault for receiuing it at the hands of the vnfaithful, though their parents and frendes that offer them vnto ſuch to be baptized, be in no ſmal fault. S. Hierom to Damaſus Pope of Rome, ⁴compareth that See to the Arke, & them that communicate vvith it, to them that vvere ſaued in the Arke: al other Schiſmatikes and Heretikes, to the reſt that vvere drowned. \fq ¹ \ft Noës Arke & the vvater, a figure of christs Crosse & Baptisme. \fq ² \ft \+it Ibid. c.17\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Baptisme receiued of Heretikes or Schismatikes, vvhen damnable, vvhen not. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ep. 57\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.21 \fq The examination of a good conſcience. \ft ¹ The Apoſtle ſeemeth to allude here to the very forme of Catholike Baptiſme, conteining certaine interrogatories and ſolemne promiſes made of the articles of the Chriſtian faith, and of good life, and of renouncing Satan and al his pompes and vvorkes. vvhich (no doubt) hovvſoeuer the Caluiniſts eſteeme of them, are the very Apoſtolike ceremonies vſed in the miniſtration of this Sacrament. \+it See S. Denys in fine Ec. hierarchiae. S. Cyril li.12 in Io. c.64. S. Auguſtine ep.23. S. Baſil de Sp. ſancto c.12 and 15. S. Ambroſe de ijs qui myſterijs initiantur c.2.3.4\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The ceremonies of Baptisme, namely \+it Abrenuntio &c\+it*.\ef*
\v 22 vvho is on the right hand of God, ⊢ ſvvallovving death, that vve might be made heires of life euerlaſting: being gone into heauen, Angels and Potentates and Povvers ſubiected to him.
\c 4
\cd That they arme them ſelues to ſinne no more after Baptiſme, againſt the tentations of the Heathen, conſidering that the general end novv approcheth. 8 ſpecially tovvard their euen-Chriſtians to ſhevv their charitie, hoſpitalitie, and grace, doing al to the glorie of God. 12 And as for being perſecuted becauſe they are Chriſtians, to reioyce, conſidering the revvard that they ſhal haue vvith Chriſt, and damnation that they auoid thereby.
\p
\v 1 \sc CHRIST\sc* therfore hauing ſuffered in the fleſh, be you alſo armed vvith the ſame cogitation. becauſe he that hath ſuffered in the fleſh, hath ceaſed from ſinnes:
\v 2 that novv not after the deſires of men, but according to the vvil of God he liue the reſt of his time in the fleſh.
\v 3 For the time paſt ſufficeth (to accompliſh the vvil of the Gentiles) them that haue vvalked in riotouſnes, deſires, exceſſe of wine, banketings, potations, and vnlavvfnl ſeruices of Idols.
\v 4 VVherein they maruel blaſpheming, you not concurring into the ſame confuſion of riotouſnes.
\v 5 vvho ſhal render account to him, vvhich is ready to iudge the liuing and the dead.
\v 6 For, for this cauſe alſo vvas \f 1 \fr 4.6 \ft It hath the ſame difficulty and ſenſe that the other like vvordes haue before, \+it Chap. 3\+it*. See the annotation there \+it v. 19\+it*. and S. Aug. \+it ep. 69\+it*, & Oecumenius \+it vpon this place\+it*.\f* it euangelized to the dead: that they may be iudged in deede according to men, in the fleſh: but may liue according to God in the Spirit.
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 4.7 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Sunday next after the Aſcenſion.\f* And the end of al \f 2 \fr 4.7 \fq `ſhal approche \ft \+it `is at hand\+it*.\f* `ſhal approche'. Be vviſe therfore: and vvatch in praiers.
\v 8 But before al things, hauing mutual charitie continual among your ſelues: becauſe \x - \xt Prov. 10.\x* charitie couereth the multitude of ſinnes. \ef - \fr 4.8 \fq Charitie couereth. \ft ¹ Faith onely cannot iuſtifie, ſeeing that charitie alſo doth cauſe remiſsion of ſinnes. And ſaying charitie, he meaneth loue and charitable vvorkes tovvard our neighbours, vnto vvhich vvorkes ² of mercie the Scriptures do ſpecially attribute the force to extinguiſh al ſinnes. See S. Auguſtine \+it c. 69 Enchiridij and tract. 1. in ep. 1. Io. c. 1\+it*. and venerable Bede \+it vpon this place\+it*. And in the like ſenſe the holy Scriptures commonly commend vnto vs almes and deedes of mercie for redemption of our ſinnes. \+it Prouerb. c. 10. Eccleſiaſtici 12. v. 2. Danielis c. 4. v. 24\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith. \fq ² \ft Works of mercy.\ef*
\v 9 \x - \xt Ro. 12, Heb. 13.\x* Vſing hoſpitalitie one tovvard an other vvithout murmuring.
\v 10 \x - \xt Ro. 12, 6.\x* Euery one as he hath receiued grace, miniſtring the ſame one tovvard an other: as good diſpenſers of the manifold grace of God.
\v 11 If any man ſpeake, as the vvordes of God. if any man miniſter: as of the povver, vvhich God adminiſtreth. that in al things God may be honoured by \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt: ⊢ to vvhom is glorie and empire for euer and euer. Amen.
\v 12 My deereſt, thinke it not ſtrange in the feruour vvhich is to you for a tentation, as though ſome nevv thing happened to you:
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 4.13 \ft The Epiſtle for ſome Martyrs.\f* but communicating vvith the paſſions of Chriſt, be glad, that in the reuelation alſo of his glorie you may be glad reioycing.
\v 14 \x - \xt Mt. 5, 11.\x* If you be reuiled in the name of Chriſt, you ſhal be bleſſed: becauſe that vvhich is of the honour, glorie, and vertue of God, and the Spirit vvhich is his, ſhal reſt vpon you.
\v 15 But let none of you ſuffer as a murderer, or a theefe, or a railer, or a coueter of other mens things.
\v 16 But if as a Chriſtian, let him not be aſhamed, but let him glorifie God in this name.
\v 17 for \x - \xt Hier. 25, 29.\x* the time is that iudgement begin of the houſe of God. And if firſt of vs: vvhat ſhal be the end of them that beleeue not the Goſpel of God? \ef - \fr 4.17 \fq That iudgement begin. \ft ¹ In this time of the new Teſtament, the faithful and al thoſe that meane to liue godly (ſpecially of the Clergie) muſt firſt and principally be ſubiect to Gods chaſtiſement and temporal afflictions, vvhich are here called iudgement. VVhich the Apoſtle recordeth for the comfort and confirmation of the Catholike Chriſtians, vvho vvere at the time of the vvriting hereof, excedingly perſecuted by the heathen Princes & people. \fq ¹ \ft The better men most afflicted in this life.\ef*
\v 18 And \x - \xt Prov. 11, 31.\x* if the iuſt man ſhal ſcarſe be ſaued: where ſhal the impious and ſinner appeare? \ef - \fr 4.18 \fq If the iuſt. \ft ¹ Not that a man dying iuſt and in the fauour of God, can afterward be in doubt of his ſaluation, or may be reiected of God: but that the iuſt being both in this life ſubiect to aſſaults, tentations, troubles, and dangers of falling from God and loſing their ſtate of iuſtice, & alſo oftentimes to make a ſtraite count, & to be temporally chaſtiſed in the next life, cannot be ſaued vvithout great vvatch, feare, and trembling, and much labouring and chaſtiſement. ² And this is far contrarie to the Proteſtants doctrine, that putteth no iuſtice but in faith alone, maketh none iuſt in deede and in truth, teacheth men to be ſo ſecure and aſſured of their ſaluation, that he that hath liued vvickedly al his life, if he onely haue their faith at his death, that is, if he beleeue ſtedfaſtly that he is one of the elect, he ſhal be as ſure of his ſaluation immediatly after his departure, as the beſt liuer in the vvorld. \fq ¹ \ft The iust man him self is hardly saued. \fq ² \ft Against the vaine securitie of only faith.\ef*
\v 19 Therfore they alſo that ſuffer according to the vvil of God, let them commend their ſoules to the faithful creator, in good deedes. ⊢
\c 5
\cd He exhorteth Prieſtes to feede their flockes, onely for Gods ſake and revvard of heauen, vvithout al lordlines. 5 the laie to obey: al to be humble one tovvards an other. 8 to be conſtant in the Catho. faith, conſidering it is not man, but that lion the Diuel that perſecuteth them, 9 as he doth the vvhole Church alſo, & that God vvil after a vvhile make them ſecure in heauen.
\p
\v 1 \sc THE\sc* \f 1 \fr 5.1 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Apollinaris \+it July 23\+it*.\f* ſeniors therfore that are among you, I beſeche, my ſelf a fellovv ſenior vvith them and a vvitneſſe of the paſſions of Chriſt, vvho am alſo partaker of that glorie vvhich is to be reuealed in time to come: \ef - \fr 5.1 \fq Seniors. \ft ¹ Though the Latin, \+it Senior\+it*, be not appropriated to holy order by vſe of ſpeache, neither in the Latin nor in our language: yet it is plaine that the Greeke vvord \+it Preſbyter\+it*, vvhich the Apoſtle here vſeth, is here alſo (as commonly in other places of the new Teſtament) a vvord of Eccleſiaſtical office, and not of age, and is as much to ſay as \+it Prieſt\+it* or \+it Biſhop\+it*. For the Apoſtle him ſelf being of that order, ſpeaketh (as by his vvordes it is plaine) to ſuch as had charge of ſoules, ſaying, \+it Feede the flocke of God vvhich is among you\+it*. Becauſe vve folow the vulgar latin tranſlation, vve ſay \+it Seniors and Senior\+it*: vvhereas othervviſe vve might and ſhould ſay according to the Greeke, \+it The Prieſts therfore I beſeech, my ſelf a fellovv-prieſt vvith them\+it*. So doth S. Hierom read \+it (Preſbyteros compreſbyter\+it*) and expound ep. 85. So tranſlateth Eraſmus, and Beza him ſelf. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Senior\+it* in the vulgar translation is often Priest or Bishop. \+it See Act. 15\+it*.\ef*
\v 2 feede the flocke of God vvhich is among you, \f 1 \fr 5.2 \ft \+it c ἐπισκοποῦντες.\+it*\f* cprouiding not by conſtrainte, but vvillingly according to God: \f 2 \fr 5.2 \ft Deſire of lucre, or to exerciſe holy functions for gaine, is a filthy fault in the Clergie, and therfore much to be auoided.\f* neither for filthie lucre ſake, but voluntarily:
\v 3 neither as ouerruling the Clergie, but made examples of the flocke from the hart. \ef - \fr 5.3 \fq Ouerruling. \ft ¹ Not ſuperiority, preeminence, ſoueraintie, or rule on the one ſide, nor obedience, ſubiection, and inferiority on the other ſide, be forebidden in the Clergie: but tyrannie, pride, and ambitious domination be forbidden, and humility, meekenes, moderation are commended in Eccleſiaſtical Officers. the Greeke vvord here of ruling or ouerruling, ²being the ſame that our Sauiour vſeth in the Goſpel ³ of the tyrannical rule of ſecular Heathen Princes, ſaying to his Apoſtles, that it ſhal not be ſo among them: according as here the prince of the Apoſtles teacheth his brethren the Eccleſiaſtical rulers. \fq ¹ \ft Not superioritie but tyrannie and lordlines is forbidden in the Clergie. \fq ² \ft \+it κατακυριεύοντες.\+it* \fq ³ \ft κατακυριεύοντες. \+it Mat. 20. v. 25\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.3 \fq The Clergie. \ft ¹ Some of the Engliſh nevv tranſlations turne it corruptly, \+it Pariſhes\+it*: others, \+it heritages\+it*: both, to auoid the moſt knovven, true, and common vvord in al Chriſtian languages, to vvit, ² \+it Clergie\+it*, a vvord, by vſe of al antiquity, & agreably to the holy Scriptures, made proper to the Spiritualty or Clergie. though in an other more vulgar acception it may agree to al Chriſts choſen heritage, as vvel of lay people as Prieſts. vvhich the Proteſtants had rather folovv, becauſe they vvill haue no difference betvvene the laity & the Clergie. But the holy fathers far othervviſe euen from the beginning. Vvhereof ſee S. Cyprian ep. 4. 5. 6. &c. And S. Hierom ep. 2. to Nepotianus c. 5. vvhere he interpreteth this vvord. \+it Therfore\+it* (ſaith he) \+it Clericus\+it*, that is, \+it a Clergie man, vvhich ſerueth the Church of Chriſt, let him firſt interprete his name, and the ſignification of the name being declared, let him endeuour to be that vvhich he iſ called\+it*. If \+it κλῆρος\+it* (Clerus) \+it in Greeke, be called in Latin, Sors, therfore are they called Clerici, that is, Clergie men, becauſe they are of the lot of our Lord, or becauſe our Lord him ſelf is the lot or portion of Clergie men &c\+it*. Vvhich calling no doubt vvas taken out of the holy Scriptures, \+it Numer. 18\+it*. and \+it Deutero. 18\+it*. vvhere God is called the inheritance, ³lot, and portion of the Prieſts and Leuites: and novv vvhen men be made of the Clergie, they ſay, \+it Dominus pars hæreditatis meæ\+it*. that iſ, \+it Our Lord is the portion of mine inheritance\+it*. but ſpecially out of the nevv Teſtament, Act. 1, 17. 25. and 8, 21. Vvhere the lot or office of the Eccleſiaſtical miniſterie is called by this vvord \+it κλῆρος\+it*, Clerus. See in Venerable Bede the cauſes vvhy this holy ſtate being ſeuered by name from the Laity, doth vveare alſo a crovvne ⁴ on their head for diſtinction. \+it Lib. 5. hiſt. Angl. c. 22\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Heret. translation. \fq ² \ft The name of Clergie and Clerke. \fq ³ \ft \+it κληρονομια. κλῆρος. μερὶς.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Priests crovvnes.\ef*
\v 4 And vvhen the prince of paſtors ſhal appeare, you ſhal receiue the incorruptible crovvne of glorie. \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq Crovvne of glorie. \ft ¹ As life euerlaſting ſhal be the revvard of al the iuſt, ſo the preachers & Paſtors that doe vvel, for their doing ſhal haue that revvard in a more excellent degree, expreſſed here by theſe vvordes, \+it Crovvne of glorie\+it*. according to the ſaying of Daniel c. 12. \+it They that ſleepe in the duſt of the earth, ſhal avvake, one ſort to life euerlaſting, others to euerlaſting rebuke. but ſuch as be learned, ſhal ſhine as the brightnes of the firmament: and ſuch as inſtruct many to iuſtice, ſhal be as ſtarres, during al eternitie\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The heauenly crovvne of Doctors and preachers.\ef*
\v 5 In like maner ye yong men be ſubiect to the ſeniors. And do ye al inſinuate humilitie one to an other, becauſe \it God reſiſteth the proude: and to the humble he giueth grace\it*.
\v 6 \x - \xt Prov. 3. Iac. 4. Iac. 4. Pſa. 54. Mt. 6, 25.\x*\f 2 \fr 5.6 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 3 Sũday after Pentecoſt.\f* Be ye humbled therfore vnder the mightie hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of viſitation:
\v 7 caſting al your carefulnes vpon him, becauſe he hath care of you.
\v 8 Be ſober and vvatch: becauſe your aduerſarie the Diuel as a roaring lion goeth about, ſeeking vvhom he may deuoure.
\v 9 vvhom reſiſt ye, ſtrong in faith: knovving that the ſelf ſame affliction is made to that your fraternitie vvhich is in the vvorld.
\v 10 But the God of al grace, vvhich hath called vs vnto his eternal glorie in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*, he vvil perfite you hauing ſuffered a litle, and confirme, and ſtabliſh you.
\v 11 To him be glorie and empire for euer and euer. Amen. ⊢
\v 12 By Syluanus a faithful brother to you, as I thinke, I haue breefely vvritten: beſeching and teſtifying that this is the true grace of God, vvherein you ſtand.
\v 13 The Church ſaluteth you, that is in Babylon, coëlect: and Marke my ſonne. \ef - \fr 5.13 \fq That is in Babylon. \ft ¹ The Proteſtants ſhevv them ſelues here (as in al places vvhere any controuerſie is, or that maketh againſt them) to be moſt vnhoneſt and partial handlers of Gods vvord. The aûcient fathers, namely S. Hierom \+it in Catalogo de ſcriptoribus Eccleſiaſticis, verbo Marcus\+it*: Euſebius \+it li. 2 c. 14 hiſt. Oecumenius\+it* vpon this place: and many moe agree, that Rome is meant by the vvord Babylon, here alſo as in the 16 and 17 of the Apocalypſe: ſaying plainely, that S. Peter vvrote this Epiſtle at Rome, ² vvhich is called Babylon for the reſemblance it had to Babylon that great citie in Chaldæa (vvhere the Ievves vvere captiues) for magnificence, Monarchie, reſort and confuſion of al peoples and tongues, and for that it vvas before Chriſt and long after, the ſeate of al Ethnike ſuperſtition & idolatrie, & the ſlaughter houſe of the Apoſtles & other Chriſtian men, the Heathen Emperours the keeping their cheefe reſidéce there. See S. Leo \+it Ser. 1 in Nati. Petri & Pauli\+it*. This being moſt plaine, and conſonant to that vvhich folovveth of S. Marke, vvhom al the Eccleſiaſtical hiſtories agree to haue been Peters ſcholer at Rome, and that he there vvrote his Goſpel: ³ yet our Aduerſaries fearing hereby the ſequele of Peters or the Popes ſupremacie at Rome, deny that euer he vvas there, or that this Epiſtle vvas vvritten there, or that Babylon doth here ſignifie Rome: but they ſay that Peter vvrote this Epiſtle at Babylon in Chaldæa, though they neuer reade either in Scriptures or other holy or profane hiſtorie, that this Apoſtle vvas euer in that tovvne. but ſee their ſhameles partiality. here Babylon (ſay they) is not taken for Rome, becauſe it vvould folovv that Peter vvas at Rome &c. but in the Apocalypſe vvhere al euil is ſpoken of Babylon, there they vvill haue it ſignifie nothing els but Rome, and the Romane Church alſo, not (as the fathers interprete it) the temporal ſtate of the Heathen Empire there. So do they folovv in euery vvord, no other thing but the aduantage of their ovvne hereſie. See the Annotation vpon the laſt of the Romans v. 16. and vpon the 17 of the Apocalypſe. v. 5. ⁴ And as for their vvrangling vpon the ſupputation of the time of his going thither, and the number of yeres that he vvas there, & the diuerſitie that ſeemeth to be in the Eccleſiaſtical vvriters concerning the ſame, read B. Fiſher and other that ſubſtantially anſvver al ſuch cauils. And if ſuch contentious reaſoning might take place, vve ſhould hardly beleeue the principal things recorded either in Eccleſiaſtical hiſtories, or in the Scriptures them ſelues. Concerning the time of Chriſts fleing into Ægypt, of the comming of the Sages to adore him, yea of the yeres of his age, & time of his death, al aûcient vvriters do not agree. and concerning the day of his laſt ſupper and inſtitution of the holy Sacrament, there is diuerſitie of opinions. Shal vve therfore inferre that he neuer died, and that the other things neuer vvere? ⁵ Can the Heretikes accord al the hiſtories that ſeeme euen in holy Scripture to haue contradiction? Can they tel vs certainely, vvhen Dauid firſt came to Saul, and the like? doubt they vvhether the vvorld vvas euer created, becauſe the count of the yeres is diuers? Do they not beleeue that Paradiſe euer vvas, becauſe no man knovveth vvhere it is? and ſuch other like things infinite to rehearſe? Vvhich vvhen they vvere done, vvere plaine and knovven things in the vvorld: and novv for vs to call them to an account, after ſo many yeres, ages, and vvorldes, is but ſophiſtication and plaine infidelitie. And this ſect of the Proteſtants ſtanding onely vpon deſtruction, and negatiues, & dealing vvith our religion euen as Iulian, Porphyrie, and Lucian did, it is an eaſie thing for them to beſtovv their time in picking of quarels. \fq ¹ \ft S. Peter vvriteth from Babylon, that is, Rome. \fq ² \ft Vvhy Rome vvas called Babylon. \fq ³ \ft The Protestants vvil haue Babylon to signifie Rome in other places but not here. \fq ⁴ \ft The Protestants vvrangle about the time of Peters being at Rome. \fq ⁵ \ft Many things most true (euen in the Scriptures) are not agreed vpon concerning the time.\ef*
\v 14 \x - \xt Ro. 16, 16. 1. Cor. 16, 20. 2. Cor. 13, 12.\x* Salute one an other in a holy kiſſe. Grace be to al you vvhich are in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*. Amen.
